  "response": "I've created `/app/filter.py`. Run it as `python3 /app/filter.py file.html` and it removes the JavaScript from the file in place. It uses only the Python standard library.\n\n**How it works:** it reads the HTML the same way a browser does and copies everything through unchanged except the dangerous parts. A clean file comes out byte-for-byte identical, including whitespace, line endings and odd character encodings. If nothing needs removing, the file isn't rewritten.\n\n**What it removes:**\n- **Whole elements, with their content:** `<script>` and `<iframe>`.\n- **Tags only, keeping what's inside:** `<object>`, `<embed>`, `<applet>`, `<frame>`.\n- **Event-handler attributes:** any `on*` attribute such as `onerror` or `onload`, plus `srcdoc`, and any attribute or tag name that isn't valid.\n- **Attributes with `javascript:` or `vbscript:` values.** This includes disguised forms such as HTML entities, `&colon;`, or tabs and control characters inside the word.\n- **`data:` URLs** in link/source attributes, unless they are ordinary images (png, gif, jpeg, webp and similar). A `data:` URL in a `<meta>` refresh is always removed.\n- **CSS that can run script:** `expression(`, `behavior:`, `-moz-binding` or `javascript:` in a `style` attribute (the attribute goes) or in a `<style>` block (the whole block goes). Encoded or commented-out versions are caught too.\n- **Comments that hide script,** such as old Internet Explorer conditional comments.\n- **Tricks that rely on parsing quirks:** for example, an `<img onerror>` hidden inside `<svg><style>` or `<noscript>`, or a `<script>` tag rebuilt from pieces once another part is cut out. The filter runs repeatedly until the output stops changing.\n\n**Testing:**\n- A clean test page with tables, headers, a form, a `<style>` block, comments and entities came out byte-for-byte identical. So did a file with CRLF line endings and non-UTF-8 bytes; only an injected `onerror` was removed.\n- I ran about 40 known attack strings through the filter, and in the container's headless Chromium:\n  - 10 of the originals pop an alert as soon as the page loads.\n  - None of the filtered files do.\n- Many of the other vectors only run when someone clicks or hovers, which this check doesn't trigger. I confirmed by reading the output that those were removed.\n\n**Trade-offs:**\n- The `javascript:` check covers every attribute. A harmless `title=\"learn javascript: basics\"` would be removed as well.\n- An `<iframe>` with an ordinary `http` address is still removed entirely, because it would load another page's scripts.\n\nI removed my temporary test files from `/app`, so `filter.py` is the only file there.",
